/-!
# Erdős Problem 118: development of the ordinal counterexample

The concrete positive relation `Partition lambda lambda 3` is proved in
`Positive`, using the isolated, locally rebuilt complete 591 proof.
The negative relation `¬ Partition lambda lambda 6` is proved in `Negative`,
with an explicit graph on the correct ordered good-sequence carrier.
The sharper requested relation `¬ Partition lambda lambda 5` is proved in
`NegativeFive`, using Larson's eleven-segment graph. Its full-order density
and five-clique exclusion are independently proved. The theorems below
give the exact positive-three/negative-five pair and disprove the
universal implication. The earlier six-target is preserved separately.

The remaining paragraphs are the history of the preserved task-native
alternative game route. Its unfinished strict cases do not serve as
assumptions in the independently completed positive proof.

`Erdos118.BoundedNeighborhoods` proves the positive conclusion under a
uniform bound on all neighborhood order types, and shows that any putative
counterexample must have full-order sets of vertices above every finite
neighborhood-rank bound. The unbounded case is still required.

`Erdos118.RamseyGame` proves the countable Ramsey game dichotomy and the
two-completion triangle case. The concrete height-two game is constructed
below. Its outside triangle argument is now proved; the inside triangle
argument remains unproved. The full-order-copy construction from initial
red outcomes is proved in `RedFamily`.

`Erdos118.CoordinateModel` proves that literal good words supported in any
infinite coordinate alphabet still have exact order type `lambda`.
`Erdos118.WordResponses` proves that complete words form an unavoidable thin
response family and applies the two-completion theorem to the concrete model.
This gives red separated tails, not independence of a full-order copy.
`Erdos118.PartialWordResponses` supplies actual completion responses after
any valid partially filled body, with fresh coordinates above any bound.
The interleaved decision rules are formalized below; synchronization remains missing.

`Erdos118.RootInterleaving` proves inside and outside synchronization for
games that cut each word after its root. Both blue outcomes force a triangle,
and both games have red outcomes on a common infinite alphabet for a
triangle-free graph. Multiple interior cuts and full-order assembly remain.
`Erdos118.SynchronizedRounds` extends the outside synchronization argument
to any fixed finite number of response rounds, when both sides use the same
protocol and there are no architect choices. It also handles a countable
collection of these protocols on one alphabet. Adaptive stopping rules remain.

`Erdos118.ConservativeAlphabet` fuses every completed-finite-support bound
on one infinite alphabet without changing the model's order type. It gives
one full-type family on which every separated pair is red. It does not
infer full independence or encode arbitrary new architect choices by support.

`Erdos118.RootAssembly` derives the local triangle-free product relation
and proves that a full-type family with red pairs between different roots
contains an independent copy of `lambda`. Constructing that family from an
arbitrary triangle-free graph is still required; separated pairs alone do
not satisfy its global-pair hypothesis.

`Erdos118.StructuralThinning` constructs a full-type family with disjoint
supports at different roots. Its explicit triangle-free shift graph has an
edge on every entire alphabet-supported model, but an independent full copy
after this structural thinning. Thus alphabet-only thinning cannot give the
missing global-pair conclusion for arbitrary triangle-free graphs.

`Erdos118.InteriorWords` gives explicit fresh extensions from interior leaves:
advance to a specified leaf count or later body, or complete the word. Each
records exact concatenation, root preservation, and freshness above any bound.
`Erdos118.PrefixOrder` identifies nested shortlex with literal-word lexicographic
order and gives injective chronological prefix codes.

`Erdos118.PrefixRealization` assembles those moves by strong recursion over
prefix codes. Its completed outputs form an order-embedded copy of the raw
model, with exact type `lambda`. Different-root supports are disjoint, and
every nontrivial cut made by another root's coordinate ends at an interior
position. This construction has no coloring hypothesis and supplies no
clear-pair node labels. Compatible labels, conservativity for the actual
multiple-cut game, and its blue-to-triangle proof remain required.

`Erdos118.LabelledExtensions` constructs decorated prefixes with labels
chosen before their actual markers. It proves label deletion, fresh root and
body creation, label-preserving advances, and complete literal outputs.
`Erdos118.LabelledFrames` retains the selected indices and unused slots and
constructs every live parser transition above any finite bound, with exact
ordinary and decorated suffix equations. These are local transitions, not
a thin response-family theorem. Its stronger transition theorem explicitly
preserves existing root labels and the prefix of created body labels.

`Erdos118.LabelledRealization` tracks decorated blocks in one chronological
construction while comparing nonempty ordinary blocks for the ordinal order.
Its underlying range has exact type `lambda`. Different-root decorated
supports are disjoint; every nontrivial threshold cut in both streams is one
pending labelled frame, whose annotations persist into the completed word.
These are structural results; they do not assign colors.

`Erdos118.LabelCoarsening` projects stored labels to those used by actual
pair cuts. It retains the ordinary words, both threshold-cut streams, and
the selected body/leaf indices. Every retained annotation has a cut witness.
The full clear-pair geometry and adaptive response protocol are supplied
below. Full-order conservativity and the multiple-cut triangle argument remain required.

`Erdos118.BodyResponses` gives an unavoidable thin response family at a
fixed body-decision stem. Its fixed label size and first label coordinate
determine the stopping rule, and its finite support determines the resulting
position uniquely. This does not yet define the complete adaptive game.

`Erdos118.RootResponses` supplies the exact root-setup front, stopping at a
stem before the first selected body's label is chosen. Its composition with
a body response produces the expected pending frame. `StemResponses` fills
to a prescribed body count, including final completion, with the exact list
of retained and empty labels. `LeafResponses` supplies fixed-length leaf
advances and consumes a pending slot. All these fronts are proved thin and
unavoidable.

`Erdos118.DecisionStates` connects the response fronts, preserves words and
annotations, and proves well-foundedness of transitions on either word.
`Erdos118.AdaptiveGame` restricts responses above both current words and
constructs a well-founded adaptive game for a parameterized terminal payoff.
The Ramsey dichotomy applies to it. The exact clear-pair graph payoff is
defined below; the blue-to-triangle theorem and conservative full-order
realization remain unproved. The game construction is not the positive relation.

`Erdos118.CutIndices` proves uniqueness of ordinary interior parsing and
identifies both projected label lists with the intrinsic numerical cut
indices of the actual pair. Ordinary words and disjoint decorated supports
are retained.

`Erdos118.ClearPairs` proves label/node separation from joint cuts and
constructs exact clear annotations for both outputs at different roots.
`Erdos118.GraphPayoff` defines the inside/outside payoffs on these actual
annotated words and the given graph. Terminal red certificates in both
orientations imply a red edge. Conservative realization from initial red
certificates and the initial blue-to-triangle theorem remain unproved.

`Erdos118.ConservativeRuns` extracts finite response guards from red
certificates and proves red preservation along concrete finite runs meeting
those guards. It does not assert the required full-order family of runs.

`Erdos118.SkippedCuts` proves that fixed numerical cut indices determine
one prefix of a word. Two consecutive responses on one side after reaching
a selected leaf prevent any later clear completion. `BlueRuns` extracts
concrete completed plays from blue certificates on infinite alphabets and
therefore forces a handoff after each newly reached selected leaf. A new
labelled body opposite a completed word is impossible for a blue outcome.
These restrictions do not yet synchronize three pairwise games.

`Erdos118.WholeBlue` proves the whole-first triangle case by decoding the
restricted fronts into the established two-completion game. A triangle-free
graph's remaining initial blue command therefore has a positive root label.
`LabelOverlays` constructs fresh label lists with a shared last/first entry
and actual root/body response overlays preserving the underlying word.
`ExactSlots` proves that reachable states retain every unused label: at an
empty slot list, the current selected index is the actual last label.
The later modules prove the outside adaptive positive-root-label case.
The inside case remains unproved.

`ReservedResponses` constructs actual root/body responses with a second label
reserved before the common marker and proves root/body/final-completion
decoder identities. `EndpointOrder` tracks support and endpoints through
concrete runs and rules out blue outside play after the second word completes
before the first. A blue pending word against a completed word has no unused
slots. `SecondWhole` proves the whole-second triangle case by using one common
completion against a far blue pair. The remaining blue branches therefore
have positive root labels on both words. The later outside synchronization
uses these commands; the inside case remains open.

`BlueReservations` applies reserved root/body fronts to actual blue response
certificates and retains the required handoffs. `BlueCheckpoints` constructs
blue continuations stopped at the last selected body or leaf on either side,
with new coordinates in any infinite subalphabet. `CompletionReplay` proves
that every literal completion supplies an exact final response with the old
annotations and unchanged suffix. Blue final-response bounds can therefore
be applied to ordinary completions produced elsewhere, provided those bounds
precede the whole suffix. These interfaces do not synchronize the three runs.

The stopping induction now exposes an actual final response from a
noncheckpoint predecessor; its last-leaf corollaries therefore retain the
opposite-side handoff. `BoundaryRelays` proves that a pending continuation
in the last selected body keeps its stem, marker, and body label. Exact
last-slot identities construct the reserved root/body response fronts.
`BlueRelays` derives initial-root relays from actual bounded histories and
constructs a two-game last-body fork on either original side. Both games
retain blue certificates and use the same ordinary position at the shared
boundary. Installing reservations at both words' boundaries in the needed
order is handled by the following coupled construction.

`PreparedRelays` separates one-response preparation from use at the last
leaf and supports either target side. Its stored body bound lies below the
body marker, so later entries automatically retain it. `ManagedRelays`
keeps root reserves before the last body and prepared data within that body;
blue responses preserve this invariant until both words have empty unused
lists. `CoupledRelays` constructs the finite coupled run and starts it from
actual positive root responses. It yields both original last pending words
and their two blue first-pending relays with matching ordinary words. The
outside common third word and final triangle are supplied below. The inside
case and full-order conservative red realization remain unproved.

`OutsideEndgame` proves that a last right leaf cannot finish before an
unfinished left word, and that its left handoff forces a final left command.
`RightRelays` constructs the common third word using a fixed second-root
certificate and preparation on the right side of both games.
`OutsideTriangle.initial_not_blue` proves that a triangle-free graph has
no initial blue certificate in the outside game: complete the first word
once, replay its literal suffix in the original game, and complete the
other two words on a tail above both resulting edge bounds. All three
edges have the same actual literal vertices. This closes the outside
adaptive triangle case, not the positive partition relation.

`InsideEndgame` proves the opposite endpoint restriction directly: the
right word must finish first. At a last left leaf, a right blue command
must therefore be a final completion from a last pending right word.
`LeafReplay` restores the old labels on a same-body ordinary extension
and decodes it as an exact selected-leaf response. Both side certificates
apply when the entire new suffix exceeds the original announced bound.
`SharedLast` constructs two body labels with a shared last entry and buffers
an actual body response inside an earlier selected-leaf response.
`InitialRelays` transports the last right word to the first left word of a
fresh game, using one fixed working alphabet. `InsideCompletion` joins the
three literal completions once their shared pending positions are supplied.
`InsideSingleton.no_singleton_root_certificate` constructs these positions
and excludes initial inside blue commands with a singleton root label.
`initial_root_setups_at_least_two` records the remaining case exactly.
The general `InsideEndgame.last_right_pending_cases` checkpoint restricts
the left handoff at a last right leaf to one remaining selected leaf or
one remaining selected body. In the body case, the following body command
has a singleton label. These local alternatives do not align the earlier
root labels of three games.
`CompletionReplay.setup_of_literal_stem` restores old annotations on an
ordinary extension to any later completed-body count; final completion
is now its preserved special case. This provides the literal interface
needed for a buffered next-body response. `StemReplay.left_body_words`
now applies the exact old blue command to that unchanged suffix and
returns the actual next-body setup with its old annotations.
`RootBuffer` constructs and transports shared-last root reservations,
then begins a fresh root response inside the old next-body response.
The old whole-front and later suffix bounds are kept separate.
`BodyReplay` now prepares, carries, and fires the body response while
retaining the target's exact older decorations. It only requires equality
of ordinary stems. `ReplaySources` constructs the target commands from
two concrete kinds of actual uniform front certificates. `RetainedController`
preserves different source data on the two words and constructs an actual
finite run to both last leaves, retaining and firing both preparations.
`InsideRootBody.triangle_of_reserve` instantiates this controller and
proves the triangle at the remaining-body checkpoint with its shared-last
root reservation. The other remaining-leaf alternative for a nonsingleton
initial root command is not covered by that theorem.
`BlueCheckpoints.right_last_leaf_handoff_entry` retains the actual entry's
non-body alternative, and `InitialRelays.root_to_last_first_nonbody` rules
out a left body decision at its returned checkpoint. Using these facts,
`InsideRootLeaf.initial_remaining_leaf` constructs the reserved checkpoint
from an arbitrary initial inside blue certificate in a triangle-free graph
and eliminates its remaining-body alternative by the proved triangle.
It retains the precise remaining-leaf data, including the initial root
certificate, exact slots, shared-last root reserve, and ordinary freshness.
`CommonFirst` constructs two actual root or body fronts with the same first
ordinary position and prefix-comparable labels. `JointOpening.respond_bodies`
uses both actual blue commands and guards to obtain matching child positions.
`JointOpening.initial_opening` constructs three compatible first pending blue
pairs, retaining exact slots, the initial root certificate, and freshness.
Their continuation through unequal unused lists is not proved; these first
positions are not substituted for the last positions in `InsideCompletion`.
`JointMoves` now joins the actual selected-leaf and next-body responses when
their next selected counts agree, retaining both old annotation sets and
both conservative guards. Its next-body-and-body result composes actual
steps on one alphabet. `JointFinish` proves that a common completed right
vertex gives a triangle by completing the remaining blue pair above two
neighborhood bounds. Thus the joint last-right completion branch is proved
without requiring the other two words already at last leaves. The unequal
remaining-list cases and full joint continuation are still unproved.
`JointAlignment` now classifies the exact remaining lists of two aligned
versions and proves a strict decrease for matching moves. `JointController`
cycles through the three ready words, preserving actual runs and all blue
certificates, until different response kinds occur. Its final-matching branch
is excluded by the proved triangle. The unequal-response checkpoint is
constructed from the initial blue game, but is not yet excluded.
`FreshCheckpoints` stops blue runs above an extra coordinate bound while
keeping the same alphabet for every conservative guard. `CurrentBody` stops
at the current body's last selected leaf without assuming empty root slots.
`BufferedLeaf` replays the whole fresh extension as a previously announced
selected-leaf response, preserving both actual histories and blue handoffs.
This local two-game replay does not preserve or exclude the third pair.
`ReversedForks` now runs TU to its last selections while keeping two S
prefixes fixed, firing the retained right-word relays into ST and SU.
`OverlapFinish` fixes the old U completion bound before a new SU extension.
`LeafForkTriangle` and `RootForkTriangle` prove the two last/next overlap
triangles using that bound, exact replay, and retained body preparation.
The root case permits further coarse ST root slots. `FreshBodyCheckpoint`
supplies the required stopped run to a last body marker. Constructing an
appropriate initial S overlap is not supplied by these conditional triangles.
`RootOverlap` constructs two actual initial root fronts with a common first
selected body and a fine-last/coarse-next root index. `FirstBody` uses them
to exclude uniformly singleton first-body certificates. Nash--Williams then
gives an infinite root pool on which zero-parameter body certificates on H
are absent and positive-parameter certificates exist. The initial root
certificate is retained. This leaves the nonsingleton first-body case, not
the full inside exclusion or positive relation.
`FiniteGuards` proves that a finite coordinate support permits only finitely
many actual states, then thins an alphabet to dominate both guards and the
pair bound for numerically bounded commands. Its anchored form retains the
original guard alphabet. `FrameAnchors` supplies preceding-sibling root and
size markers in the existing chronological realization. `SizedExtensions`
and the strengthened `LabelBudget` retain label sizes, creation phases, and
new-label coordinates before choosing each frame. The old public interfaces
